{"name":"Wild Ginger","hex":"#825059","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wild ginger","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the many plants similar to culinary ginger (Zingiber officinale) in appearance or aroma.\nin family Zingiberaceae, especially:\nAny of the Alpinia species (shell gingers), especially Alpinia caerulea.\nCurcuma australasica (native turmeric)\nHedychium gardnerianum (kahili ginger, ginger lily)\nSiphonochilus aethiopicus (African ginger)\nAny of the numerous Zingiber species\nRenealmia aromatica (rat plantain)\nAny of several species of Asarum, especially Asarum caudatum (British Columbia wildginger), Asarum canadense, and Asarum sieboldi.","in family Zingiberaceae, especially:\nAny of the Alpinia species (shell gingers), especially Alpinia caerulea.\nCurcuma australasica (native turmeric)\nHedychium gardnerianum (kahili ginger, ginger lily)\nSiphonochilus aethiopicus (African ginger)\nAny of the numerous Zingiber species\nRenealmia aromatica (rat plantain)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wild_ginger"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wild ginger","blocks":[{"text":"Wild ginger may refer to any of a variety of plants, often with a similar appearance, odour or taste to cultivated ginger. Species involved include:"},{"items":["Any of the Alpinia species, especially A. caerulea, a large tropical flowering plant","Any of the Asarum species, especially A. caudatum, a groundcover with kidney- or heart-shaped leaves and a small maroon flower and ginger-scented roots; native to North America","Curcuma australasica, \"Australian turmeric\"","Hedychium gardnerianum, Himalayan plant with fragrant yellow spiked flowers","Siphonochilus aethiopicus, \"African ginger,\" a rhizome with a showy purple flower with a yellow center","Any of the numerous Zingiber species"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wild_ginger"},{"title":"Wild Ginger (restaurant)","blocks":[{"text":"Wild Ginger is a restaurant with multiple locations in the Seattle metropolitan area, in the U.S. state of Washington. The business was established in 1989 and has been recognized by the James Beard Foundation for its wine program."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Seattle_-_Mann_Building_and_Washington_Mutual_Center.jpg/330px-Seattle_-_Mann_Building_and_Washington_Mutual_Center.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wild_Ginger_(restaurant)"},{"title":"Ginger","blocks":[{"text":"Ginger is a flowering plant whose rhizome, ginger root or ginger, is widely used as a spice and a folk medicine. It is an herbaceous perennial that grows annual pseudostems about one meter tall, bearing narrow leaf blades. The inflorescences bear flowers having pale yellow petals with purple edges, and arise directly from the rhizome on separate shoots."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Koeh-146-no_text.jpg/330px-Koeh-146-no_text.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginger"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Ginger_inflorescence.jpg/500px-Ginger_inflorescence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Ginger_inflorescence.jpg/1280px-Ginger_inflorescence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Ginger_inflorescence.jpg/500px-Ginger_inflorescence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGinger_inflorescence.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Flower_of_Ginger1.jpg/500px-Flower_of_Ginger1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, http